 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "COX'S CAVE AND GOUGH'S CAVE. Stalactites, a very chilly 11 degrees, and the 10,000-year-old Cheddar Man. Mind your head. The roof is older than your gran."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "CHEDDAR GORGE. Britain's biggest gorge, cliffs up to 137 m. Jacob's Ladder climbs it in 274 steps. The view at the top is free. The wheeze is extra."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "THE TOP OF THE GORGE. Peregrine falcons nest on these cliffs, and feral goats graze them. The goats are not impressed by you. The peregrines are."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE STRAWBERRY LINE. The old railway carried Cheddar strawberries to the cities. The trains are gone. The strawberries are not."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "CHEDDAR. The cheese is named after this village. West Country Farmhouse Cheddar was once matured in these caves, where it's always cool."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "WOOKEY HOLE. The River Axe wells up from deep inside the Mendips, and the cave mouth is across the water at the foot of the cliff. You'll want to wade: Brook-wade comes with the Urban Stone from Keeper Ray in Liverpool. The witch under the arch doesn't mind, but her stalagmite does."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 6,
   "plaque": "mendips_rock_of_ages",
   "title": "Rock of Ages",
   "text": "The story goes that the curate Augustus Toplady sheltered from a storm in a cleft of rock at Burrington Combe, a few miles north of here, and was moved to write the hymn Rock of Ages. The rock still stands beside the road, and still keeps the rain off."
